    "file": "Categories/01. Location/01.02 Martinaise/01.02.03 The Martinaise Coastal Boardwalk.MD",
    "title": "The Martinaise Coastal Boardwalk",
    "text": "The Martinaise Coastal Boardwalk is a long, weathered wooden promenade stretching along the western shoreline of the Martinaise district overlooking the cold expanse of the Bay of Revachol. Constructed over a century ago during the Belle Epoque of the Revacholian monarchy, the boardwalk was designed as an elegant pedestrian concourse where wealthy tourists strolled past fashionable boutiques, pastry kiosks, and decorative gazebos while admiring the open waters of the Insulindian Isola.\n\nThe walkway is elevated above the tidal mudflats on heavy creosote-treated timber pilings and reinforced granite seawalls. Decades of harsh salt spray, relentless winter gales, and total civic neglect have severely degraded the woodwork, leaving rotting planks, missing handrails, and warped floorboards that creak beneath pedestrian traffic. The decorative street lamps and ornate iron balustrades installed during the reign of King Frissel IV have rusted to dark husks, while shuttered carnival stalls and abandoned souvenir kiosks stand peeling along the landward margin. Prominent along the central concourse stands the riderless bronze statue of King Filippo III, its imperial horse frozen in mid-gallop while the monarch's likeness was pulled down and melted during the Revolution of '02.\n\nIn the contemporary era, the boardwalk functions as a windswept thoroughfare connecting central Martinaise with the western fishing shacks and the canal lock bridge. Neighborhood retirees and off-shift dockworkers gather near the rusted iron railings to observe maritime traffic, smoke cheap Astra cigarettes, and watch the Coalition naval aerostats cruising silently across the overcast horizon. During severe winter squalls, freezing sea spray crashes over the seawall, forming hazardous ice crusts that render passage treacherous for local residents.",
